  "articleBody": "Upgrading your back entrance for accessibility can feel like a complex project, and it's easy to stumble into some common pitfalls. Knowing what to watch out for can save you time and headaches later. Here are some frequent mistakes and how to avoid them: First off, *assuming a 'back door' is automatically exempt*. Just because it's not the main entrance doesn't mean it gets a free pass. Always assess who uses it and for what purpose. If employees regularly use it, or if it's the only route to an altered area, it likely needs upgrades. Another common error is *overlooking employee use*. Many folks only think about public access, but employee access is just as crucial. If your staff uses that back entrance, it needs to be considered. Then there's the mistake of *failing to evaluate whether path-of-travel improvements may be required*. If you're altering a 'primary function area,' you might need to make other entrances accessible as part of the overall project, even if they aren't directly connected to the main work. You also need to prioritize a clear door width, appropriate threshold height, and continuous accessible routes. Don't forget that if there's an alternate entrance, it should be usable independently and during the same hours as any main entrance. When these elements get complicated, especially with ramp design, door hardware, or signage, don't hesitate to consult with an expert. They can help ensure your project is fully compliant and truly accessible.",
